== Prerequisites ==

* From [[ψ Dispersion Relations]] (A3): the linearised wave equation <math>(\Box - m_{\rm eff}^2)\psi = 0</math> and the relation <math>\omega^2 = k^2 + m_{\rm eff}^2</math>.
* From [[ψ Impedance Theory]] (A4) (for Robin condition only): the impedance ratio <math>\alpha_R = Z_0/Z_{\rm wall}</math>.

== Why Boundaries Are Not Optional ==

The Helmholtz equation (time-harmonic form of the wave equation) inside a closed region <math>V</math>:

:<math>(\nabla^2 + k^2)\psi = 0, \qquad k^2 = \omega^2 - m_{\rm eff}^2</math>

admits infinitely many solutions for any real <math>k</math>. Without a boundary condition specifying the behaviour of <math>\psi</math> on the surface <math>\partial V</math>, the solution is not unique. A boundary condition selects a countably infinite but discrete set of eigenvalues <math>k_{nlm}</math> — the resonant wave numbers — and corresponding eigenfunctions <math>\psi_{nlm}(\mathbf{x})</math> — the resonant mode shapes. Every [[ψ Resonator]] and [[ψ Waveguide]] is a designed eigenvalue problem.

== The Three Fundamental Boundary Types ==

=== Dirichlet — Perfect ψ Absorber ===

:<math>\psi\bigl|_{\partial V} = 0 \qquad \text{(field vanishes at wall)}</math>

'''Physical meaning:''' The boundary is a perfect ψ absorber. The ψ field has a node (zero) at every point on the wall — no ψ energy reflects, all is absorbed. Equivalent in EM to a perfect electric conductor (PEC) for the scalar field.

'''Material requirement:''' A material with very high ψ loss tangent (imaginary part of <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math> dominates over real part): <math>\tan\delta_\psi \gg 1</math>. ψ energy incident on the boundary is converted to heat or disorder rather than reflected.

'''Mode shape consequence:''' Every resonant mode inside a Dirichlet cavity has a node at the wall. The lowest mode has a single antinode at the centre. Mode frequencies are higher than in a Neumann cavity of the same size (nodes at walls push energy toward the centre, increasing effective wave number).

'''Engineering applications:'''
* [[ψ Shield / Absorber]] (C8): outer wall — Dirichlet condition ensures no ψ escapes the shield.
* [[Psi Defender]] outer shell: Dirichlet termination suppresses all incident ψ.
* [[ψ Detector]] (C10): absorbing boundary used to prevent reflected ψ from contaminating measurement.

=== Neumann — Perfect ψ Reflector ===

:<math>\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial n}\bigg|_{\partial V} = 0 \qquad \text{(normal gradient vanishes at wall)}</math>

where <math>\hat{n}</math> is the outward normal to <math>\partial V</math>.

'''Physical meaning:''' Zero normal ψ flux — no ψ energy escapes through this face. The field has an antinode (maximum) at the wall; all energy reflects. Equivalent in EM to a perfect magnetic conductor (PMC).

'''Material requirement:''' A material with Im(<math>\mu_\psi</math>) ≈ 0 and Re(<math>\mu_\psi</math>) ≫ 1 — a perfect ψ magnetic mirror. In practice, the ferrofluid channels in the [[TPMS Lattice Design]] approximate this condition at the Stabilizer's target frequency when the H-field is set to produce maximum <math>\mu_{\rm eff}</math>.

'''Mode shape consequence:''' Resonant modes have antinodes at the wall. The lowest mode has its peak at the boundary rather than the centre, producing a field distribution that maximises the ψ field at the operator's skull surface — the design intent for the [[Psi Stabilizer]].

'''Engineering applications:'''
* [[ψ Resonator]] (C1): Neumann inner wall maximises stored ψ energy; ψ does not leak.
* [[Psi Stabilizer]] shell: Neumann condition at inner surface produces ψ₀₁₀ mode with field peak at temporal lobes.
* [[ψ Waveguide]] (C2): Neumann condition at guide walls supports low-loss guided modes.

=== Robin — Impedance Wall ===

:<math>\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial n}\bigg|_{\partial V} + \alpha_R\,\psi\bigg|_{\partial V} = 0</math>

'''Physical meaning:''' Partial absorption proportional to field amplitude. The Robin condition continuously interpolates:
* <math>\alpha_R \to 0</math>: Neumann limit (no absorption, perfect reflection).
* <math>\alpha_R \to \infty</math>: Dirichlet limit (total absorption).

The coefficient <math>\alpha_R</math> is the ratio of the ψ wave impedance in free space to the wall impedance:

:<math>\alpha_R = \frac{Z_0}{Z_{\rm wall}} = \frac{1/\omega^2}{Z_{\rm wall}}</math>

from [[ψ Impedance Theory]] (A4). Engineering the wall material's <math>Z_{\rm wall}</math> — by adjusting the ratio <math>\mu_\psi/\varepsilon_\psi</math> — directly sets <math>\alpha_R</math>.

'''Engineering applications:'''
* Outer surface of [[Psi Stabilizer]]: Robin condition allows controlled leakage for [[HRV as ψ Proxy]] sensing (the [[OPM Magnetometry for ψ Detection]] sensor must detect the field through the shell).
* [[ψ Coupler]] (C5): Robin wall at the coupling gap sets the coupling coefficient <math>\kappa</math>.
* [[ψ Filter]] (C6): frequency-selective Robin walls (frequency-dependent <math>\alpha_R</math>) implement bandpass response.

