== Mode Quantisation ==

For any finite cavity with a specified boundary condition, the Helmholtz equation yields a countably infinite discrete spectrum of eigenvalues. The general form:

:<math>\omega_{nlm}^2 = m_{\rm eff}^2 + k_{nlm}^2</math>

where the wave numbers <math>k_{nlm}</math> are determined by the geometry and boundary condition. The indices <math>(n, l, m)</math> label the modes — their specific meaning depends on the coordinate system appropriate to the cavity geometry.

{{Way_It_Works
| title       = How geometry and boundary conditions jointly determine the ψ mode spectrum
| step1_label = Choose the cavity geometry
| step1_text  = The geometry of the cavity determines which coordinate system separates the Helmholtz equation. Sphere → spherical coordinates; oblate spheroid (head shape) → oblate spheroidal coordinates; cylinder → cylindrical coordinates.
| step2_label = Separate the Helmholtz equation
| step2_text  = In the chosen coordinates, <math>(\nabla^2 + k^2)\psi = 0</math> separates into a product of ordinary differential equations, one per coordinate direction. Each ODE has regular and irregular solutions; only the regular ones are physical inside the cavity.
| step3_label = Apply boundary conditions
| step3_text  = At the cavity wall, impose Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin. For spherical cavities with Neumann conditions, this requires <math>j'_l(ka) = 0</math> — the zeros of the derivative of the spherical Bessel function. For Dirichlet: <math>j_l(ka) = 0</math>.
| step4_label = Solve the eigenvalue equation
| step4_text  = The boundary condition equation (Bessel zero, oblate spheroidal eigenvalue, etc.) has a discrete set of solutions <math>k_{nlm}</math> for each index triple. These are the allowed wave numbers. The corresponding <math>\omega_{nlm} = \sqrt{m_{\rm eff}^2 + k_{nlm}^2}</math> are the resonant frequencies.
| step5_label = Compute mode shapes
| step5_text  = The eigenfunction <math>\psi_{nlm}(\mathbf{x})</math> gives the spatial pattern of the ψ field inside the cavity at frequency <math>\omega_{nlm}</math>. The mode shape determines which anatomical regions experience high ψ field intensity during device operation.
| step6_label = Select the target mode
| step6_text  = Engineering choice: which mode <math>(n,l,m)</math> should the device operate in? The choice is driven by the anatomical field distribution needed for the application. The [[Psi Stabilizer]] requires maximum field intensity at the temporal lobes — this selects the ψ₀₁₀ mode.
| step7_label = Design the cavity for that mode
| step7_text  = Given target frequency <math>\omega_{\rm target}</math> and mode <math>k_{010}</math>, the cavity dimension <math>a</math> and effective mass <math>m_{\rm eff}</math> must satisfy <math>\omega_{\rm target}^2 = m_{\rm eff}^2 + k_{010}^2</math>. This is the design equation linking geometry, ferrofluid state, and operating frequency.
}}

== The Oblate Spheroidal Cavity — Psi Stabilizer ===

=== Geometry ===

The [[Psi Stabilizer]] shell approximates a human head: an oblate spheroid with equatorial semi-axis <math>a \approx 110</math> mm and polar semi-axis <math>b \approx 90</math> mm (eccentricity <math>e = \sqrt{1 - b^2/a^2} \approx 0.57</math>). The aspect ratio <math>a/b \approx 1.22</math> is chosen to match the 95th-percentile adult head dimensions.

In oblate spheroidal coordinates <math>(\xi, \eta, \phi)</math>, the Helmholtz equation separates into the spheroidal wave equation. The radial equation is the oblate spheroidal wave equation; the angular equations are associated Legendre equations modified by the spheroidal parameter <math>c = ka\,e</math>.

=== Mode Designation ===

Modes are labelled <math>\psi_{nlm}</math>:
* <math>n</math> — radial index: number of nodal surfaces in the radial direction.
* <math>l</math> — angular index: related to the number of nodal lines on the spheroidal surface.
* <math>m</math> — azimuthal index: number of azimuthal nodal planes (0 = azimuthally symmetric).

=== The ψ₀₁₀ Mode ===

The lowest Neumann mode of the oblate spheroidal cavity — <math>\psi_{010}</math> — has the following properties:

* '''Radial structure:''' No radial nodes — field varies smoothly from centre to wall.
* '''Angular structure:''' One angular half-wavelength — field peaks at equatorial plane (<math>\theta = \pi/2</math>) and decreases toward the poles.
* '''Azimuthal structure:''' Azimuthally uniform — field is the same at all longitudes for <math>m=0</math>.
* '''Field maximum:''' The equatorial plane of the oblate spheroid corresponds anatomically to the '''temporal lobe''' region of the cerebral cortex.
* '''Target frequency:''' <math>\omega_{010}/(2\pi) \approx 40</math> kHz — within the Shepard tone operating range and above the theta-band EEG effective-frequency target.

This is not a designed-in feature — it is a mathematical consequence of the oblate spheroidal geometry with Neumann boundary conditions. The temporal lobe correspondence arises because the equatorial plane is where the lowest Neumann mode of an oblate cavity concentrates its energy. Designing the shell to match head geometry automatically places the field peak at the anatomically relevant region.

