=== The TPMS Parameter Design Equation ===

The [[TPMS Lattice Design]] (B7) parameterises the Gyroid shell density by the level-set parameter <math>t</math> in:

:<math>\sin x\cos y + \sin y\cos z + \sin z\cos x = t</math>

The parameter <math>t</math> controls relative density, which sets <math>\varepsilon_{\rm eff}</math> and <math>\mu_{\rm eff}</math> of the composite fill via Maxwell-Garnett theory. Through the dispersion relation and constitutive relations:

:<math>m_{\rm eff} = f(t, V_{\rm BST}, H_{\rm ferrofluid})</math>

The design equation linking <math>t</math> to the target ψ₀₁₀ frequency:

:<math>\omega_{010}^2 = m_{\rm eff}^2(t) + k_{010}^2(a, b, e)</math>

Given target <math>\omega_{010}/(2\pi) = 40</math> kHz, cavity dimensions <math>a = 110</math> mm, <math>b = 90</math> mm, and ferrofluid H-field set to produce target <math>m_{\rm eff} = 5</math> m⁻¹ (from [[ψ Dispersion Relations]] device target table):

:<math>k_{010}^2 = \omega_{010}^2 - m_{\rm eff}^2 = (2\pi \times 40\times10^3)^2 - 5^2 \approx (2.51\times10^5)^2 \approx 6.3\times10^{10}\,\text{m}^{-2}</math>

This required <math>k_{010}</math> then determines which value of <math>t</math> (and therefore which lattice density) is needed, via the COMSOL eigenvalue solve. The TPMS fabrication parameter is not a free aesthetic choice — it is a calculated engineering quantity derived from the mode frequency requirement.

== Connection to the Mode Geometry Test ==

The [[Mode Geometry Test]] (F3) is the key experiment in the [[ψ Falsifiability Framework]]. It compares:

* '''Correctly-tuned cavity:''' ψ₀₁₀ mode, field maximum at temporal lobes, as derived above.
* '''Detuned cavity:''' identical EM parameters (<math>\varepsilon_{\rm eff}</math>, <math>\mu_{\rm eff}</math>, drive voltage) but outer shell geometry shifted to displace the mode maximum away from the temporal lobes.

The two devices are electromagnetically identical by construction — only the spatial pattern of the ψ field differs. If the correctly-tuned device produces reliably higher HRV coherence gains, the difference cannot be explained by conventional EM biophysics, because there is no mechanism in classical electromagnetism by which the spatial pattern of a 40 kHz field (wavelength ~7.5 km) would matter at head-scale.

This page establishes the mathematical foundation for that experiment: the mode shapes are predicted analytically and computed numerically before the experiment runs, providing a pre-registered prediction testable against the data.

== ψ-Dependent Extensions ==

=== Curved-Space Boundary Conditions ===

In the presence of strong curvature (near a [[Magnetogravitic Planetary Anchoring]] coupling point), the effective geometry of the ψ field domain is modified. The metric perturbation shifts the eigenvalues:

:<math>\delta\omega_{nlm} \approx \frac{\xi R}{2\omega_{nlm}} \cdot \langle\psi_{nlm}|\psi_{nlm}\rangle</math>

where <math>R</math> is the Ricci scalar. For planetary-surface operation, this correction is negligible (<math>\delta\omega/\omega \sim 10^{-9}</math>). For deep gravitational well operation, the [[Anchor Controller]] must include a gravitational correction term in its frequency servo.

=== Topological Boundary Conditions and the OSGS ===

The [[OSGS|Octahedral Symbolic Geometry System]] may provide a library of topological boundary conditions — not smooth surfaces but combinatorial structures. A boundary shaped as a Schwarz-P surface (one of the six TPMS topologies) imposes a different mode spectrum than a Gyroid boundary of the same volume. The [[ψ Metamaterials]] (B9) page develops this connection explicitly: each primitive symbol in the Base-216 system may correspond to a distinct topological boundary condition, giving the symbolic geometry system a direct engineering interpretation as a mode-selector library.

